WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.220
So your family had a garden?

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:03.980
Oh, yeah.

3
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.140
That was every year.

4
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.320
Chickens . . . We didn't buy chickens.

5
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.760
We raised chickens.

6
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.560
I done killed so many chicken by wringing their neck.

7
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.120
And see what they would do is, when they get ready . . . The chicken was all over the yard,

8
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.200
but when they get ready to eat one they put them in the coop.

9
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.000
I guess that's what they called, clean them out for so many days.

10
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.780
Then our job was to go out there and kill them and wring the neck of them.

11
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.780
They showed me how to pick them.

12
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.700
The same thing with the ducks.

13
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.980
Of course them guineas, I never could catch them.

14
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.360
I don't remember dealing with guineas.

15
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:45.560
But I tell you something else that always stick in my mind, I guess there's about nature.

16
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.240
They had plum trees, fruit trees.

17
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.960
You ain't never heard of . . . You heard of Muscadine [grapes]?

18
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:52.860
Muscadine wine, yeah.

19
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.500
Well we used to go in . . . these muscadines, would go in the woods and collect them.

20
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:02.240
Well, we had fig trees, peach trees,

21
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.480
and oh . . . I guess eight or nine plum trees and they were, I mean, big old healthy plums.

22
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.620
And all of a sudden this stuff just disappeared, and I think I know why.

23
00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.040
That's when industry started coming in.

24
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:27.560
But just about everything that we . . . except for sugar and stuff like that you’d go to the store and get . . . and it might be interesting to know,

25
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.680
you might want this in your . . . the big interview.

26
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.380
They had one grocery store in Mossville.

27
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.120
Only one.

28
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.820
There was Garrett’s on that right there by the railroad track.

29
00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.920
Garrett's Grocery Store.

30
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:39.990
Garrett's Grocery Store?

31
00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.060
Ira Garrett.

32
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.580
And you could go there and get sausage . . . Anything in the house.

33
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.140
And what he used to do to get by on credit.

34
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.890
You’d go there and they’d write it on the book and they pay it.

35
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.260
Now, many times I had to go to the store with that bill to collect.

36
00:01:54.260 --> 00:02:00.000
And that was the only store that served the whole Mossville.

37
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.660
And by that same area, they had a post office one time in Mossville.

38
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.700
Across the street from it.

39
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.280
And right by the grocery store were the railroad tracks.

40
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.080
They had a passenger train,

41
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.660
and some people caught the train right there off of the . . . Get on the train and ride to Texas,

42
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.260
or wherever they had to go.

43
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:19.620
Baton Rouge.

44
00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.860
There were some other businesses, too, they have in Mossville besides that.

45
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.840
They had a drugstore in Mossville one time.

46
00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:28.760
Purdy’s Drugstore.

47
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:37.140
Now it was a sub . . . I’d say a sub-drugstore from the one she had in Lake Charles [Louisiana] on Mill Street.

48
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:44.260
Then she opened one over here right down the street from where the school is now, where Sasol [energy and chemical company] is.

49
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:50.720
Now talk about clubs . . . they had a bunch of clubs like bars and all that over in Mossville.

50
00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.000
Had a hotel in Mossville one time right down by Evergreen [Road], see.

51
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.560
So I remember a lot of things about Mossville.

52
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.520
You ever heard of Shoat’s Prairie?

53
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.020
Well, somebody going to have it somewhere.

54
00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.840
It’s up in . . . He got his name from all of the wild hogs,

55
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.840
and that’s right up there where Sasol is building some of their . . .

56
00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:12.960
What is it?

57
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.060
Shoat’s Prairie.

58
00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.320
Is a shoat a kind of a pig?

59
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:16.220
Wild hog.

60
00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.560
Wild pigs.

61
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.800
And they'd run all over the place.

62
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.060
People would kill them and eat them.

63
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.740
Some people wouldn't because they was wild.

64
00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.040
A lot of people had a real regular hogs that they would raise.

65
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.840
But these were hogs that they would run in the woods.

66
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.840
And that’s where it got its name, Shoat’s Prairie.

67
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.480
Just for my own curiosity, were the hogs dangerous?

68
00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.237
Did they have those tusks?

69
00:03:40.237 --> 00:03:40.960
Well yeah!

70
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.840
Yeah they was dangerous.

71
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:43.600
They'll cut you.

72
00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.700
Don't get too close to them, see.

73
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:50.060
Most people were the ones that had guns but they'd trap them, see.

74
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.320
And then they would shoot them.

75
00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:58.060
But today I think that's what they do today, don't they trap them?

76
00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.080
They still got them in this area and they got them all around this area.

77
00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.740
In Carlyss [Louisiana], Moss Bluff [Louisiana], but they’re real dangerous.
